Here the merge does not use a second array. When the next value from the right half is smaller, it is saved, the values between the two positions are shifted right, and the saved value is inserted.

That keeps the sorting work inside the original array, but the shifting can be costly. The code still follows the usual divide, sort, and merge pattern, so it is useful when avoiding a separate buffer matters more than speed.

```csharp
class Solution
{
    public int[] mergeSort(int[] nums)
    {
        if (nums == null || nums.Length < 2)
        {
            return nums;
        }

        Sort(nums, 0, nums.Length);
        return nums;
    }

    private void Sort(int[] nums, int left, int right)
    {
        if (right - left <= 1)
        {
            return;
        }

        int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
        Sort(nums, left, middle);
        Sort(nums, middle, right);
        Merge(nums, left, middle, right);
    }

    private void Merge(int[] nums, int left, int middle, int right)
    {
        int i = left;
        int j = middle;

        while (i < j && j < right)
        {
            if (nums[i] <= nums[j])
            {
                i++;
                continue;
            }

            int value = nums[j];
            int index = j;

            while (index > i)
            {
                nums[index] = nums[index - 1];
                index--;
            }

            nums[i] = value;
            i++;
            j++;
            middle++;
        }
    }
}
```

The input array is changed directly. Empty arrays, one-item arrays, and already sorted ranges work without special merge operations.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case because inserting a value may shift many items.
Space complexity: `O(log n)` for the recursive calls, with `O(1)` extra space apart from that.

This one avoids recursion. It starts by treating every single value as a sorted part, then merges neighboring parts of length 1, 2, 4, and so on.

The merge range is clipped at the array size, which takes care of the last part when the length is not a power of two. An empty array and an array with one value are returned without any work.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> mergeSort(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(nums.size());
        vector<int> buffer(n);

        for (int width = 1; width < n; width *= 2) {
            for (int left = 0; left < n; left += 2 * width) {
                int middle = min(left + width, n);
                int right = min(left + 2 * width, n);
                int i = left;
                int j = middle;
                int k = left;

                while (i < middle && j < right) {
                    if (nums[i] <= nums[j]) {
                        buffer[k++] = nums[i++];
                    } else {
                        buffer[k++] = nums[j++];
                    }
                }

                while (i < middle) {
                    buffer[k++] = nums[i++];
                }

                while (j < right) {
                    buffer[k++] = nums[j++];
                }
            }

            nums.swap(buffer);
        }

        return nums;
    }
};
```

Each pass combines sorted blocks into larger sorted blocks. The array is modified, and the returned vector is the same sorted data.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the second array.

Instead of always splitting at the middle, this version first finds already sorted runs. For an input such as `[1, 2, 6, 3, 4, 8]`, it sees `[1, 2, 6]` and `[3, 4, 8]`, then merges those runs.

The dry run for `[3, 4, 1, 2]` creates two runs, `[3, 4]` and `[1, 2]`. One merge produces `[1, 2, 3, 4]`. If the input is already sorted, there is only one run and no merge pass is needed.

```go
func mergeSort(nums []int) []int {
    if len(nums) < 2 {
        return nums
    }

    runs := make([][]int, 0)
    start := 0

    for index := 1; index <= len(nums); index++ {
        if index == len(nums) || nums[index] < nums[index-1] {
            run := make([]int, index-start)
            copy(run, nums[start:index])
            runs = append(runs, run)
            start = index
        }
    }

    for len(runs) > 1 {
        nextRuns := make([][]int, 0, (len(runs)+1)/2)

        for index := 0; index < len(runs); index += 2 {
            if index+1 == len(runs) {
                nextRuns = append(nextRuns, runs[index])
                continue
            }
            nextRuns = append(nextRuns, mergeRuns(runs[index], runs[index+1]))
        }

        runs = nextRuns
    }

    return runs[0]
}

func mergeRuns(left []int, right []int) []int {
    merged := make([]int, 0, len(left)+len(right))
    i := 0
    j := 0

    for i < len(left) && j < len(right) {
        if left[i] <= right[j] {
            merged = append(merged, left[i])
            i++
        } else {
            merged = append(merged, right[j])
            j++
        }
    }

    merged = append(merged, left[i:]...)
    merged = append(merged, right[j:]...)
    return merged
}
```

This returns a new sorted slice and leaves the input values untouched. It can be faster on partly sorted data, while a fully unsorted input still has the usual merge-sort behavior.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` in the worst case, and close to `O(n)` when the input is already one sorted run.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

A top-down version keeps splitting the array until each part has one item. Then the two sorted parts are joined into the buffer, and copied back into the original array.

For example, `[4, 1, 3, 2]` becomes `[4, 1]` and `[3, 2]`, then `[1, 4]` and `[2, 3]`, and finally `[1, 2, 3, 4]`. The same buffer is reused for every merge, so the code does not create a new temporary array each time.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] mergeSort(int[] nums) {
        if (nums == null || nums.length < 2) {
            return nums;
        }

        int[] buffer = new int[nums.length];
        sort(nums, buffer, 0, nums.length - 1);
        return nums;
    }

    private void sort(int[] nums, int[] buffer, int left, int right) {
        if (left >= right) {
            return;
        }

        int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
        sort(nums, buffer, left, middle);
        sort(nums, buffer, middle + 1, right);
        merge(nums, buffer, left, middle, right);
    }

    private void merge(int[] nums, int[] buffer, int left, int middle, int right) {
        int i = left;
        int j = middle + 1;
        int k = left;

        while (i <= middle && j <= right) {
            if (nums[i] <= nums[j]) {
                buffer[k++] = nums[i++];
            } else {
                buffer[k++] = nums[j++];
            }
        }

        while (i <= middle) {
            buffer[k++] = nums[i++];
        }

        while (j <= right) {
            buffer[k++] = nums[j++];
        }

        for (int index = left; index <= right; index++) {
            nums[index] = buffer[index];
        }
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the reusable buffer and `O(log n)` recursion stack. The input array is changed in place.

